18. REALISTIC INTERACTIONS

The following examples describe expected behavior, not fixed transcripts to hardcode.

Example A — Browser diagnostics

User: “My browser is being really slow lately.”

Gamma identifies a performance troubleshooting intent.

It checks relevant browser information available without new permissions.

Gamma asks:

“Does the slowdown affect one website or most websites?”

User: “Mostly YouTube.”

Gamma narrows the issue to video playback or website-specific performance.

User: “Only when I watch videos.”

Gamma recommends relevant checks and explains which observations are verified.

User: “That didn’t help.”

Gamma remembers which step was attempted and advances to another appropriate diagnostic action.

Example B — Contextual follow-up

User: “What’s my browser?”

Gamma provides the detected browser family with the appropriate evidence classification.

User: “Is it private?”

Gamma interprets “it” in relation to the previous browser.

User: “Compare it with Firefox.”

Gamma gives an appropriate comparison without requiring the original subject to be repeated.

Example C — Device limitations

User: “How much RAM is my laptop using right now?”

Gamma explains that a normal website cannot directly read system-wide RAM usage.

It provides a relevant platform-specific method to inspect memory use manually.

It does not fabricate a value.

Example D — Multiple questions

User: “Tell me my screen resolution and whether my browser supports WebGPU.”

Gamma performs both supported checks.

It returns separate observations in a clean, readable response.

Example E — Semantic matching

User: “Every time I have a bunch of pages open, everything becomes choppy.”

Gamma should recognize that this may be a browser performance or tab-memory issue even without the words “browser lag.”

If confidence remains low, ask a useful clarification.

Example F — Incorrect premise

User: “Why is my browser definitely infected?”

Gamma should not confirm an infection without evidence.

It should ask about the observed symptoms and suggest appropriate security checks.

Example G — Web retrieval

User: “Search Wikipedia for WebAssembly.”

If web retrieval is enabled, Gamma should fetch a supported public source and provide an accurately attributed explanation.

If retrieval is disabled, Gamma should explain the available offline knowledge and offer the web option.

